{
 "id": "2017-lee-neighborhood",
 "name": "Neighborhood Satisfaction Survey (17-item NQLS scale)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the neighborhood satisfaction scale developed for the Neighborhood Quality of Life Study (NQLS; about 2,200 adults aged 20–65 in 32 neighborhoods in two regions of the United States, 2001–2005) and analysed in Lee, Conway, Frank, Saelens, Cain & Sallis (2017), \"The relation of perceived and objective environment attributes to neighborhood satisfaction\", Environment and Behavior 49(2), 136–160. Participants rate how satisfied they are with 17 aspects of their own neighborhood on a 5-point scale from strongly dissatisfied to strongly satisfied: highway access, public transportation, commuting time, shopping, friends and people known in the neighborhood, ease and pleasantness of walking and of bicycling, schools, entertainment, safety from crime, amount and speed of traffic, traffic noise, food stores, restaurants, and the neighborhood as a good place to raise children and to live. Instruction, item wording and scale labels are taken word for word from the NQLS survey on the authors' website (drjimsallis.com). No images are needed; every item is a standard single-choice question, so the platform's charts work directly. Analysis: item means and an overall satisfaction score, compared between neighborhoods or related to perceived (e.g. NEWS) and objective environment measures. Differences from the original: (1) answers are tapped on screen instead of writing a number on a line, and the printed example item is left out; (2) the authors' scoring notes could not be retrieved, so no score is computed by the page; (3) age and gender questions are added by us. The template is taken down if the authors object.",
 "author": "Lee, Conway, Frank, Saelens, Cain & Sallis",
 "year": "2017",
 "category": "Academic Research",
